    "product_id": "b92a0921-1c54-43ed-8236-5849e0fc9b3b",
    "text": "\n000\nFXUS65 KVEF 070500\nAFDVEF\n\nArea Forecast Discussion\nNational Weather Service Las Vegas NV\n900 PM PST Fri Nov 6 2026\n\n.KEY MESSAGES...\n\n*  Breezy northerly winds are expected today and Saturday, with\n   the strongest winds with elevated wave heights in the Colorado \n   River Valley. \n\n* Quiet weather with near to above normal temperatures expected \n  through at least next Thursday \n\n&&\n\n.DISCUSSION...Through Next Friday.\n\nAn extended stretch of relatively quiet and warm weather is expected \nfor the Lower Great Basin and Mojave Desert through at least next \nThursday. The only \"active\" weather to mention are breezy conditions \ntoday and tomorrow, specifically for the Colorado River Valley where \nLake Wind Advisories are in effect. \n\nA sharp upper level trough shifts southeast today placing the region \nin an area of increasing northerly flow, leading to dry conditions \nand breezy north winds today and tomorrow. The surface pressure \ngradient is not overly impressive and widespread gusts 20-30 mph \ngusts with stronger gusts due to terrain enhancement in the Colorado \nRiver Valley are expected. While Laughlin/Bullhead City may briefly \nsee 45 MPH winds, northerly gusts in the Colorado River should \nremain around 30-35 MPH. Impacts to boating due to winds and \nbuilding waves are expected on Lake Mohave and Lake Havasu and a \nLake Wind Advisory is in effect through Saturday afternoon.\n\nFor the rest of the weekend and into next week, a building ridge and \na Polar jet retreating northward will keep the storm track far away \nfrom the region. Guidance continues to show above normal upper level \nheights with this ridge as well as surface to 850mb temperature \nanomalies well above normal for this time of year. This translates \nto high temperatures in the 70s to low 80s and lows in the 50s \ngenerally for desert valleys. Cooler conditions expected for higher \nelevations but still above normal. A weak wave of energy passes \nthrough the ridge early next week; however sensible impacts to \nsurface weather conditions will be minimal as a warm and dry airmass \nremains in place. Expect the ridge to re-strengthen over the Desert \nSouthwest mid next week ahead of the next potential storm system. \nForecast confidence is fairly high through the period with strong \nagreement among ensemble and deterministic guidance. \n\nForecast confidence falls going into the following weekend as a \nstrong trough approaches the west coast. Since this system is more \nthan a week away, expect model disagreement and run to run \ninconsistencies this far out. While confidence is higher in an \noverall pattern change next weekend into the end of the month \nfeaturing troughing and possible chances for precipitation, \nconfidence is lower on the exact details and evolution of that \npattern. \n\n&&\n\n.AVIATION...For Harry Reid...For the 06Z Forecast\nPackage...Northwesterly winds around 8KT this evening will become\nlight and variable overnight before shifting to the northeast \naround 8KT by late morning. Winds will follow typical daily \ndirectional patterns, shifting to the southwest after sunset and \nbecoming light through the end of the forecast period. VFR \nconditions with clear skies will continue.\n\n\nFor the rest of southern Nevada, northwest Arizona and southeast\nCalifornia...For the 06Z Forecast Package...Across the region\ntonight, winds for most will be northwesterly with some \nvariability around 5-10KT before settling into daily directional \npatterns during the day on Saturday. The primary exceptions \ninclude KIFP and KEED in the Lower Colorado River Valley, where \nterrain-enhanced northerly winds will remain elevated and gusty \nfor most of the forecast period. Gusts to 20-25KT will be common \nfrom KIFP to KEED, increasing to 25-30KT Saturday morning through \nSaturday afternoon. Gusts diminish quickly at KEED, but persist \nthrough the evening for KIFP. VFR conditions and clear skies will \ncontinue areawide.\n\n&&\n\n.SPOTTER INFORMATION STATEMENT...Spotters are encouraged to report\nany significant weather or impacts according to standard operating\nprocedures.\n\n&&\n\n$$\n\nDISCUSSION...Moore\nAVIATION...Phillipson\n\nFor more forecast information...see us on our webpage:\nhttps://weather.gov/lasvegas or follow us on Facebook and Twitter\n",
